  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSome forecast models show a decaying thunderstorm complex dropping \nsouthward into the area Monday morning as a wave of 500-mb vorticity \nrides along the upper-level ridge. \n\nHowever, models are in much better agreement about a scattered to \nwidespread severe weather and flash flooding threat Monday night \ninto Tuesday morning as a train of 500-mb vorticity rides the ridge \nahead of a cold front. This will probably be in the form of another \nthunderstorm complex tracking from northwest to southeast from the \nGreat Lakes Monday night or early Tuesday morning. A 250-mb jet \nstreak will also be present, promoting upward motion for the \ndevelopment of thunderstorms. A southwesterly low-level jet early \nTuesday morning will bring plenty of moisture into the region, with \nmodels predicting anywhere from 2.00 to 2.25 inches of precipitable \nwater late Monday night into Tuesday.\n\nWhile it is a bit early to diagnose specific threats and locations, \nwe will need to remain on guard for potential severe thunderstorms \nand torrential rainfall during this timeframe, especially for areas \nthat were recently impacted by flooding from this past week's \nrounds of severe weather.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWith high pressure centered over the Great Plains this weekend and \nmigrating westward over the Rockies by the middle of the week, \nisolated showers and thunderstorms will be possible each afternoon. \nWith the middle Ohio Valley and the central Appalachians located \nover the periphery of the upper-level ridge, rounds of 500-mb \nvorticity will provide daily chances of showers and thunderstorms. \nNot every location will see thunderstorms each day. The main \ntimeframe of severe weather and flooding appears to be Monday night \ninto Tuesday, which was discussed in detail in Key Message 1. \nOtherwise, most thunderstorms that develop outside of that timeframe \nshould be non-severe.",
